    "summary": "Manga Hiti is a dhunge dhara, a carved stone water spout fed by an underground infiltration chamber and stone conduits, a technology dating to the Licchavi period, roughly 400 to 750 CE. Built in 570 CE at Mangal Bazar in Patan, it is considered the oldest working dhunge dhara on record, and stands at the southern corner of Patan Durbar Square. A 2019 survey recorded 573 dhunge dharas across the Kathmandu Valley's municipalities; 94 had already been lost entirely, and only 224 of the remaining 479 still produced water. Manga Hiti's own flow is not watched on any continuous schedule.",
